<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<!DOCTYPE sailpoint PUBLIC "sailpoint.dtd" "sailpoint.dtd">
<sailpoint>

  <!--
    Decides who is sent the header icon.

    The plugin declares its header snippet with
    rightRequired="ViewLoggerManagerIcon". IdentityIQ evaluates that
    server-side and does not emit the script tag at all for users who do not
    hold this right, so the icon is not hidden with CSS or removed after the
    fact - the code that would draw it is never sent.

    IdentityIQ imports everything in import/install/ when the plugin is
    installed, so this object exists as soon as the plugin does - granted to
    nobody. Add it to whichever Capability your logging administrators already
    hold. Until you do, only SystemAdministrator sees an icon, because
    IdentityIQ treats that capability as holding every right. That is
    deliberate: a plugin that starts advertising itself in every user's header
    the moment it is installed is worse behaved than one that waits to be
    asked.

    This right controls the icon and nothing else. What a user may actually do
    on the Logger Manager page is governed by the plugin's "Required
    capability" setting, which is a separate check on a separate axis. Granting
    this right does not grant access to the page - and the icon is drawn only
    for users who pass both, so holding one without the other shows nothing
    rather than a link to a refusal.

    Safe to import repeatedly: SPRight objects are keyed by name and
    independent of one another, unlike AuditConfig, which is a singleton and
    must never be shipped in a plugin.
  -->
  <SPRight name="ViewLoggerManagerIcon" displayName="View Logger Manager Icon">
    <Description>
      Show the Logger Manager icon in the IdentityIQ header. Grants no access
      to the page itself - that is controlled by the plugin's Required
      capability setting.
    </Description>
  </SPRight>

</sailpoint>
